## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to model DAG-driven work in Kubernetes and coordinate cross-namespace scheduling with a centralized DAG, while handling fairness and observability.

## Key Concepts
- DAG-based task orchestration across namespaces
- CRD and controller/operator design
- Job lifecycle, retries, backoff
- SchedulerPlugin for cross-tenant fairness
- Observability and safe cleanup

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton reconcile loop for ComputeDAG tasks
async function reconcileComputeDAG(dag) {
  const ready = dag.tasks.filter(t => t.deps.every(d => d.status === 'Complete'));
  for (const t of ready) {
    if (!t.started) spawnJob(t);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect and break cycles in the DAG at reconciliation time?
- How would you test resilience to partial failures and ensure safe rollbacks across namespaces?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests cross-namespace rollout discipline, multi-service coordination, and metric-driven promotion, a realistic pain point for large clusters.

## Key Concepts

- CRD design for multi-namespace rollouts
- Reconciliation loop and cross-resource ownership
- Safe canary/blue-green promotion with metrics gates
- Finalizers, rollback semantics, observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: reconcile loop sketch
function reconcile(rolloutGroup) {
  for (target of rolloutGroup.spec.targets) {
    ensureTrafficSplit(target, rolloutGroup.spec.strategy);
    if (metricsGateSatisfied(rolloutGroup.spec.gate)) {
      promote(target);
    } else if (shouldRollback(rolloutGroup.spec.rollback)) {
      rollback(target);
    }
  }
  updateStatus(rolloutGroup);
  removeFinalizersIfDone(rolloutGroup);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle metric scrapes outages or missing PromQL data?
- How would you test cross-namespace promotion in CI/CD pipelines?

## Why This Is Asked
Workday needs engineers who can design scalable operators that handle enterprise-scale workloads without performance degradation or resource leaks.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates will discuss: controller-runtime patterns, workqueue management, finalizers for cleanup, watch bookmarks for efficiency, backoff strategies, resource quotas, and proper event filtering to reduce reconciliation noise.

## Code Example
```typescript
func (r *MicroserviceReconciler) Reconcile(ctx context.Context, req ctrl.Request) (ctrl.Result, error) {
    // Use workqueue with rate limiting
    if !r.queue.CanAdd() {
        return ctrl.Result{RequeueAfter: time.Second * 5}, nil
    }

    // Implement finalizer pattern
    if !microservice.ObjectMeta.DeletionTimestamp.IsZero() {
        if containsString(microservice.ObjectMeta.Finalizers, myFinalizerName) {
            // Cleanup logic
            return r.cleanup(ctx, microservice)
        }
        return ctrl.Result{}, nil
    }

    // Efficient reconciliation with selective updates
    return r.reconcileWithSelectiveUpdate(ctx, microservice)
}
```

